1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,932
Multi-agent sounds like the natural next step once one agent feels limited: add a

2
00:00:04,932 --> 00:00:07,874
second one, specialised, and let them work together.

3
00:00:07,874 --> 00:00:09,627
Sometimes that's exactly right.

4
00:00:09,627 --> 00:00:14,040
Often it's reaching for more machinery before confirming the first machine was

5
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:15,398
actually the bottleneck.

6
00:00:15,398 --> 00:00:20,036
It's also, this year specifically, the architecture every vendor demo reaches for,

7
00:00:20,036 --> 00:00:24,675
because a planner coordinating three specialised workers is a genuinely impressive

8
00:00:24,675 --> 00:00:25,523
thing to watch.

9
00:00:25,523 --> 00:00:30,275
Impressive to watch and proportionate to the task are two different claims, and only

10
00:00:30,275 --> 00:00:32,651
one of them belongs in a scoping decision.

11
00:00:32,651 --> 00:00:34,518
Here's the honest starting point.

12
00:00:34,518 --> 00:00:39,270
Every handoff between two agents, one's output becoming another's input, is one more

13
00:00:39,270 --> 00:00:42,494
step in exactly the chain 6.3 taught you to multiply out.

14
00:00:42,494 --> 00:00:46,341
Splitting a task across agents doesn't remove steps from that chain.

15
00:00:46,341 --> 00:00:51,036
It usually adds one: the step where agent A's output has to be correctly understood

16
00:00:51,036 --> 00:00:51,659
by agent B.

17
00:00:51,659 --> 00:00:56,128
That's worth sitting with before anything else in this lecture, because it cuts

18
00:00:56,128 --> 00:00:58,560
against the intuition most pitches lean on.

19
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:03,255
"Two specialised agents" sounds like it should be more reliable than one generalist

20
00:01:03,255 --> 00:01:04,217
doing everything.

21
00:01:04,217 --> 00:01:08,120
Specialisation can genuinely help each agent's own per-step accuracy.

22
00:01:08,120 --> 00:01:12,646
It doesn't touch the new step created at the seam between them, and that seam is

23
00:01:12,646 --> 00:01:15,361
exactly where 6.3's maths starts counting again.

24
00:01:15,911 --> 00:01:20,517
Picture that shape built for something concrete: a planner agent breaks a request

25
00:01:20,517 --> 00:01:25,067
into research and drafting, hands each to a specialised worker, and a merge step

26
00:01:25,067 --> 00:01:27,227
reconciles both before anything ships.

27
00:01:27,227 --> 00:01:31,890
Every arrow in that diagram is a handoff, and every handoff is a new place for the

28
00:01:31,890 --> 00:01:33,369
reliability maths to bite.

29
00:01:33,369 --> 00:01:38,145
The research worker has to produce something the drafting worker, or the merge step,

30
00:01:38,145 --> 00:01:39,681
can actually use correctly.

31
00:01:39,681 --> 00:01:44,344
If the researcher's output is ambiguous and the merge step misreads it with even a

32
00:01:44,344 --> 00:01:48,779
small probability, you've added a whole new multiplication to a chain that was

33
00:01:48,779 --> 00:01:52,077
already compounding before you added a second agent to it.

34
00:01:52,077 --> 00:01:53,897
So how long is the actual chain?

35
00:01:53,897 --> 00:01:57,707
Longer than the diagram first suggests, once you count it honestly.

36
00:01:57,707 --> 00:02:02,370
The planner's own decisions, each worker's internal steps from 6.1's loop, and now

37
00:02:02,370 --> 00:02:05,895
the merge step reconciling two outputs instead of reading one.

38
00:02:05,895 --> 00:02:10,444
A task that looked like four steps as a single agent can easily be eight or nine

39
00:02:10,444 --> 00:02:15,050
once it's been split across a planner and two workers, and the whole-chain number

40
00:02:15,050 --> 00:02:17,041
from 6.3's table drops accordingly.

41
00:02:17,591 --> 00:02:21,235
Notice the left column isn't wrong about what multi-agent can do.

42
00:02:21,235 --> 00:02:25,888
It's wrong about when to find out, and finding out is expensive in exactly the ways

43
00:02:25,888 --> 00:02:26,897
that column lists.

44
00:02:26,897 --> 00:02:29,813
Local doesn't mean a permanent architectural choice.

45
00:02:29,813 --> 00:02:34,466
It means proving the handoffs actually work, on your own machine, where a bad merge

46
00:02:34,466 --> 00:02:38,727
step costs you a rerun instead of an API bill and a confused support ticket.

47
00:02:38,727 --> 00:02:43,156
Once the trace looks clean locally, moving it to production infrastructure is a

48
00:02:43,156 --> 00:02:44,278
deployment decision.

49
00:02:44,278 --> 00:02:48,763
Skipping straight to production is a debugging decision you didn't mean to make.

50
00:02:48,763 --> 00:02:53,248
"Local" here means local infrastructure and a trace you can actually read end to

51
00:02:53,248 --> 00:02:57,789
end, not a specific claim that a self-hosted model beats a hosted one on quality.

52
00:02:57,789 --> 00:03:02,387
Some teams prototype with the same hosted model they'd use in production, just run

53
00:03:02,387 --> 00:03:06,199
against local logs and a local test harness instead of live traffic.

54
00:03:06,199 --> 00:03:07,769
The point isn't which model.

55
00:03:07,769 --> 00:03:12,366
It's whether you can see every handoff clearly before real users depend on getting

56
00:03:12,366 --> 00:03:14,048
through all of them correctly.

57
00:03:14,598 --> 00:03:19,254
Say the honest caveat, because multi-agent has genuine uses and this section would

58
00:03:19,254 --> 00:03:23,115
be dishonest pretending otherwise, and it's also genuinely oversold.

59
00:03:23,115 --> 00:03:27,544
Before building the planner-and-workers version of anything, try rewriting the

60
00:03:27,544 --> 00:03:31,576
single agent's instructions more precisely, or breaking the task into a

61
00:03:31,576 --> 00:03:35,834
plan-then-execute sequence inside one agent, the pattern from last lecture.

62
00:03:35,834 --> 00:03:40,490
If that solves it, you've kept the reliability maths from last lecture and skipped

63
00:03:40,490 --> 00:03:42,421
every handoff this one just added.

64
00:03:42,421 --> 00:03:47,191
Reach for a second agent when the subtasks genuinely need different tools, different

65
00:03:47,191 --> 00:03:51,676
context, or real parallelism, not because the first attempt's prompt was vague.

66
00:03:51,676 --> 00:03:55,821
Genuine parallelism is the strongest of those three reasons, worth naming

67
00:03:55,821 --> 00:03:56,559
specifically.

68
00:03:56,559 --> 00:04:01,329
If two subtasks truly don't depend on each other's output, research and a completely

69
00:04:01,329 --> 00:04:05,985
separate compliance check, say, running them as parallel agents can save real time

70
00:04:05,985 --> 00:04:08,597
without adding a handoff on the critical path.

71
00:04:08,597 --> 00:04:13,196
That's a materially different case from splitting one sequential task into stages

72
00:04:13,196 --> 00:04:17,285
just because it felt like the architecturally sophisticated thing to do.

73
00:04:17,835 --> 00:04:22,240
That's the definition worth keeping, and the second sentence is the part most

74
00:04:22,240 --> 00:04:25,958
pitches skip: a handoff is a new step, not a free specialisation.

75
00:04:25,958 --> 00:04:30,650
Run the exercise on a real proposal, the same discipline as every other lecture in

76
00:04:30,650 --> 00:04:31,393
this section.

77
00:04:31,393 --> 00:04:36,142
If nobody can name the specific thing a single agent genuinely can't do, that's not

78
00:04:36,142 --> 00:04:37,744
a reason to say no outright.

79
00:04:37,744 --> 00:04:41,977
It's a reason to ask for the local prototype before the production budget.

80
00:04:41,977 --> 00:04:46,325
Notice this is the identical instinct from 6.1's opening test, one level up.

81
00:04:46,325 --> 00:04:50,558
There, the question was whether the model decides the next step or a human

82
00:04:50,558 --> 00:04:51,531
predetermined it.

83
00:04:51,531 --> 00:04:56,222
Here, it's whether splitting the work across agents was actually necessary or just

84
00:04:56,222 --> 00:04:58,339
felt like the more impressive answer.

85
00:04:58,339 --> 00:05:02,630
Both questions exist to stop architecture from expanding past what the task

86
00:05:02,630 --> 00:05:03,717
genuinely required.

87
00:05:04,267 --> 00:05:05,907
One thing before you move on.

88
00:05:05,907 --> 00:05:10,658
Count the handoffs specifically, the same way you counted steps for 6.3's table, and

89
00:05:10,658 --> 00:05:13,260
ask what a misread at each one actually costs.

90
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:18,011
Then ask the deployment question directly: "prototyping locally first" and "building

91
00:05:18,011 --> 00:05:22,650
straight into the production pipeline" are two different risk profiles wearing the

92
00:05:22,650 --> 00:05:24,120
same architecture diagram.

93
00:05:24,120 --> 00:05:28,815
Next lecture gives you the guardrails that apply whichever answer you get, and it's

94
00:05:28,815 --> 00:05:32,944
worth having both of today's questions answered before you walk into that

95
00:05:32,944 --> 00:05:33,679
conversation.
